@meta
id: 29-09
title: Brackets that change the answer
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: surround
par: 52
minutes: 6
vscode: click before, type a bracket, click after, type the other one, re-read it four times
open: pricing.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
The surround plugin is shipped with this lesson, same place as before.

Two functions in `pricing.ts` return the wrong number, and both bugs are
the same bug: operator precedence. Nothing has to be retyped; each one
needs a pair of brackets around a range that is already correct.

  1. `totalWithTax` should round the whole total, not just the tax rate.
     `.toFixed(2)` must apply to `subtotal + subtotal * taxRate`.

  2. `discountedTotal` should divide the discount by 100, not the percent
     alone before multiplying. `total * percent` is the part that needs
     grouping.

Two different routes are worth having in your hands here: a motion when
the range ends at something you can name, and a visual selection when it
does not.

@keys
| `ys{motion})` | surround what the motion covers with brackets |
| `t.` | up to, not including, the next dot |
| `{Visual}S{char}` | surround whatever is selected |
| `v3e` | visual, then three word-ends |

@hints
The first range ends just before `.toFixed`. There is a motion that means
"up to the next dot".
--
`yst.)` from the start of `subtotal`. For the second, select
`total * percent` visually first and then ask for the brackets.
--
`/return sub` `Enter`, `w`, `yst.)`. Then `/total \* percent` `Enter`,
`v3e`, `S)`.

@solution
    /return sub<Enter>  w  yst.)
    /total \* percent<Enter>  v3e  S)
    :wq<Enter>

`ys` takes a motion, so `t.` — a motion you learned in chapter 5 and have
used a thousand times since — becomes a way of saying "bracket this
expression up to the method call". No counting, no clicking, and if the
expression grows a term tomorrow the same keys still work.

The second one has no landmark to aim at: `total * percent` ends in the
middle of an expression with a `/` after it. That is what visual mode is
for. Select it however you like — `v3e` here, but `v` and then `f%` or
`ve` three times are all the same selection — and `S` puts the pair round
whatever is highlighted. Select, then act, instead of predicting.

That pairing is the general shape. `ys` when you can name the end of the
range, `S` when you have to look at it. Both are the same function inside
the plugin; only the way the range arrives differs.

Worth saying plainly: neither of these bugs is findable by searching. The
code compiles, the tests that exist pass, and the number is quietly wrong
by a few pence per order. You find them by reading. All the editor can do
is make the fix cheap enough that you make it the moment you see it
instead of writing a ticket.

@start pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim
" dojo-surround.vim - a small, honest surround plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   ds{char}           delete the surrounding pair
"   cs{old}{new}       change one surrounding pair into another
"   ys{motion}{char}   surround the text the motion covers
"   {Visual}S{char}    surround the selection
"
" Brackets come in pairs: ( [ { < all give you the matching closer.
" Anything else is used on both sides, so " gives "..." and ` gives `...`.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_surround')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_surround = 1

let s:pairs = {
      \ '(': '()', ')': '()',
      \ '[': '[]', ']': '[]',
      \ '{': '{}', '}': '{}',
      \ '<': '<>', '>': '<>',
      \ }

function! s:delims(char) abort
  return get(s:pairs, a:char, a:char . a:char)
endfunction

" The text between the pair, without the pair itself.
function! s:inner(char) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = ''
  silent execute 'normal! "zyi' . a:char
  let text = @z
  let @z = save
  return text
endfunction

" A visual selection covering the pair AND what is between it. vi" selects
" the inside; o h o l then walks each end out by one character onto the
" delimiters. Doing it this way rather than with va" matters: va" also
" swallows the whitespace next to the string.
function! s:select(char) abort
  return 'vi' . a:char . 'ohol'
endfunction

function! s:replace(char, text) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = a:text
  silent execute 'normal! ' . s:select(a:char) . '"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:ds(char) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:char)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  call s:replace(a:char, inner)
endfunction

function! s:cs(old, new) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:old)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let d = s:delims(a:new)
  call s:replace(a:old, d[0] . inner . d[1])
endfunction

function! s:ys(type) abort
  let d = s:delims(nr2char(getchar()))
  let save = @z
  if a:type ==# 'line'
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zy"
    call setreg('z', d[0] . "\n" . @z . d[1], 'V')
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zp"
  else
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zy"
    let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zp"
  endif
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:vs(char) abort
  let d = s:delims(a:char)
  let save = @z
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zy'
  let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> ds :<C-u>call <SID>ds(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> cs :<C-u>call <SID>cs(nr2char(getchar()), nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> ys :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>ys<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> S :<C-u>call <SID>vs(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>

@start pricing.ts
export function totalWithTax(subtotal: number, taxRate: number) {
  return subtotal + subtotal * taxRate.toFixed(2);
}

export function discountedTotal(total: number, percent: number) {
  return total - total * percent / 100;
}

@expected pricing.ts
export function totalWithTax(subtotal: number, taxRate: number) {
  return (subtotal + subtotal * taxRate).toFixed(2);
}

export function discountedTotal(total: number, percent: number) {
  return total - (total * percent) / 100;
}
